3. AGENT FRAMEWORK / OSS FRONTIER NEW =E2=80=94 TRUEFORGE

Repo: https://github.com/truefoundry/trueforge
Live primary GitHub observation: created 2026-07-23; pushed 2026-09-17
09:50Z; 5,722 stars; 441 forks; TypeScript; MIT.

*What is new:* TrueForge treats the harness itself as the product layer:
agent loop + models + MCP + git-backed skills + on-demand sandbox +
approvals + subagents + deferred tool loading + compaction + session
persistence + chat UI + HTTP/TS SDK. Local mode uses SQLite; hosted mode
adds Postgres/Redis.

*Why technically interesting:* The frontier appears to be moving from pick
an agent framework toward pick a harness/runtime that can host
interchangeable models, tools, skills and sandboxes. TrueForge is
explicitly vendor-neutral and exposes the same runtime through UI/API/SDK.

*Adoption evidence:* 5.7k stars in ~8 weeks, pushed today, external
technical coverage this week, company-backed.

*Trajectory inference:* 6m: plausible serious alternative to managed-agent
harnesses. 18m: harness layer likely commoditizes; differentiation shifts
to governance, context engineering and tool ecosystems. 36m: if standards
converge, framework names matter less than runtime
policy/observability/interoperability.

*Security implication:* TrueForge concentrates authority over MCP servers,
skills, secrets, sandboxes, session state and approvals. That makes the
harness a natural Privately enforcement point.

*Decision:* SANDBOX NOW.

*Experiment:* run one identical agent task in TrueForge and the current
Privately/QP harness. Measure tool-call count, token cost, approval
boundaries, secret exposure, session-state inspectability and ease of
inserting an authority-transition processor.
