7. DEFENDER FRONTIER NEW =E2=80=94 UBER ADR

Repo: https://github.com/uber/ADR
Paper: https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.17380

*Mechanism delta:* ADR moves beyond static prompt/MCP scanning into an
EDR-like model for agents. Its open components cover discovery,
telemetry/observability, a realistic benchmark and two-tier detection. The
sensor captures intent, tool use and execution traces across coding agents
including Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Copilot CLI, DeepSeek Harness and
opencode.

*Production evidence:* Uber reports deployment across >7,200 hosts and
>10,000 agent sessions/day in the accompanying research. ADR-Bench contains
300+ tasks, 134 MCP servers and 17 attack techniques. The paper reports
materially better detection than several existing baselines.

*What previous generation it improves on:* static signatures / prompt-only
filters / endpoint telemetry that sees the shell/file event but not the
agent causal chain.

*Dynamic/adaptive:* materially more dynamic than static scanners because it
consumes contextual execution traces and uses two-stage detection; still
not proof against fully adaptive adversaries.

*Tool outputs hostile:* benchmark explicitly includes
prompt-injection/agent attack scenarios; worth testing exact tool-output
coverage ourselves.

*Persistence/memory:* not its strongest differentiated layer. Pair with
memory-specific controls rather than assume ADR solves persistent state
poisoning.

*Decision:* ADOPT AS BENCHMARK + SANDBOX SENSOR.

*Privately edge:* ADR-Bench should become one of the external corpora
QP/probes are measured against. More importantly, its telemetry schema may
be a better starting point than inventing our own agent EDR event format.
NEW =E2=80=94 TRIPWIRE

Repo: https://github.com/neomatrix369/tripwire
Live primary observation: created 2026-08-01; pushed 2026-09-14; 10 stars /
2 forks; Python; MIT.

*Mechanism:* sandboxed multi-scanner pre-install assessment for AI
skills/MCP servers plus fail-closed guard/posture output.

*Why it survived despite 10 stars:* it directly targets the new agents
install executable/instruction-bearing capabilities supply-chain boundary.
This is exactly the type of tiny repo the scanner is supposed to notice
before popularity.

*Weakness:* adoption evidence is nearly absent and it may collapse into a
wrapper around existing scanners. Do not overrate it.

*Decision:* WATCH / QUICK CODE REVIEW, not adoption.

*Falsifier:* if its scanners are mostly delegated calls with no unique
sandbox/behavioural analysis, remove from registry priority.
